---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About Us — Private PDF Word Counter"
  description="Learn why we built the Private PDF Word Counter — an offline, in-browser document analytics tool built with zero cloud uploads."
>
  <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 py-16 sm:py-24 space-y-16">
    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="space-y-4 text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold bg-blue-500/10 text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 border border-blue-500/20">
        Our Mission
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100 leading-tight">
        Confidential Document Analytics for Everyone
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        We believe you shouldn't have to upload sensitive legal briefs, research papers, or financial filings to an unknown remote server just to count words.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Values Grid -->
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
      <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 bg-white dark:bg-neutral-900/40 space-y-3 shadow-sm">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-emerald-500/10 text-emerald-600 dark:text-emerald-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-lg">
          🔒
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">100% In-Browser Privacy</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
          Files are parsed in local browser memory via client-side Web Workers. Zero bytes ever leave your device or reach any server.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 bg-white dark:bg-neutral-900/40 space-y-3 shadow-sm">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-blue-500/10 text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-lg">
          ⚡
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">Sub-Second Processing</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
          Zero network upload latency. Documents with hundreds of pages are analyzed in fractions of a second using your device's native CPU cores.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="p-6 rounded-2xl border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 bg-white dark:bg-neutral-900/40 space-y-3 shadow-sm">
        <div class="w-10 h-10 rounded-xl bg-amber-500/10 text-amber-600 dark:text-amber-400 flex items-center justify-center font-bold text-lg">
          🆓
        </div>
        <h2 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">No Accounts, Always Free</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
          No sign-ups, no paywalls, no email collection, and no subscriptions. A dedicated single-purpose utility built for the open web.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- The Problem We Solved -->
    <section class="space-y-4 border-t border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        The Problem with Traditional PDF Converters
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
        Most web-based PDF counters operate using server-side models. When a user drops a file into their browser, the entire binary payload is transferred over the public internet to an Amazon AWS, Google Cloud, or self-hosted server cluster. There, the file is saved to a temporary directory on a shared disk, parsed by a backend script, and deleted (or retained for model training and marketing analytics).
      </p>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
        For lawyers reviewing court submissions, translators quoting proprietary manuscripts, and authors reviewing unpublished novels, this represents a completely unacceptable risk. We built <strong>PDF Word Counter</strong> to provide a trusted, zero-upload alternative where code executes exclusively within the sandbox of the user's web browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Technical Architecture -->
    <section class="space-y-4 border-t border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        How the Technology Works
      </h2>
      <p class="text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 text-sm sm:text-base leading-relaxed">
        Our utility is powered by the battle-tested Mozilla <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">pdfjs-dist</code> library, running inside an isolated HTML5 Web Worker thread. When you select a document:
      </p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm sm:text-base text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400">
        <li>The file is read as an in-memory <code class="text-xs font-mono bg-neutral-100 dark:bg-neutral-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded">ArrayBuffer</code> via the HTML5 File API.</li>
        <li>Mozilla's WebAssembly and JavaScript parsers unpack the cross-reference tables and font descriptors locally.</li>
        <li>Content streams are mapped page-by-page to reconstruct words, character lengths, and whitespace counts.</li>
        <li>When you navigate away or close the tab, the entire memory allocation is immediately freed by your browser.</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <!-- Creator & Open Source -->
    <section class="p-8 rounded-2xl border border-neutral-200 dark:border-neutral-800 bg-neutral-50/50 dark:bg-neutral-900/30 space-y-4">
      <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900 dark:text-neutral-100">
        Open Source & Verifiable
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-neutral-600 dark:text-neutral-400 leading-relaxed">
        Don't take our word for it—verify it yourself! Our codebase is fully open source. You can inspect every line of JavaScript, audit our network requests in your browser's DevTools Network tab, or deploy your own instance.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 pt-2">
        <a
          href="https://github.com/Yash-YT-tech/pdf-word-counter-secure"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener noreferrer"
          class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded-xl bg-neutral-900 hover:bg-neutral-800 text-white dark:bg-white dark:hover:bg-neutral-100 dark:text-neutral-900 text-xs font-semibold transition-all shadow-sm"
        >
          <span>View on GitHub</span>
          <svg class="w-3.5 h-3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M10 6H6a2 2 0 00-2 2v10a2 2 0 002 2h10a2 2 0 002-2v-4M14 4h6m0 0v6m0-6L10 14"></path></svg>
        </a>
        <a
          href="/"
          class="text-xs font-semibold text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline"
        >
          Try the Counter →
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
